IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pca-types`

-- Prevention of Corruption Act 1960, Part 5 (ss 23 to 25) and s 36 (protection of informers).
-- Source: source/PCA1960.txt lines 923 to 957 (Part 5), 1076 to 1095 (s 36).
--
-- The question this module answers: WHAT MAY THE COURT HEAR, AND WHAT MAY IT MAKE OF IT?  These are special rules of evidence for a
-- corruption trial.  They sit beside the Evidence Act 1893 (sg/evidence-act-1893) and do not restate it: section 25 below is the Act's
-- answer to the caution the Evidence Act's section 116 illustration (b) tells a court to bear in mind about an accomplice's evidence,
-- and section 36 is the Act's own informer rule beside the Evidence Act's sections 126 and 127.  NOTES.md section 4 lists where the two meet.

§ `23. Evidence of custom inadmissible`

-- 23: "In any civil or criminal proceeding under this Act evidence shall not be admissible to show that any such gratification as is
-- mentioned in this Act is customary in any profession, trade, vocation or calling."  The rule excludes evidence for one purpose; evidence
-- offered for another purpose is for the Evidence Act.
@ref "Prevention of Corruption Act 1960, s 23"
GIVEN e IS A `Corruption Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence of custom is made inadmissible by section 23` IF
        e's `the proceeding is a civil or criminal proceeding under this Act`
    AND e's `the evidence is offered to show that a gratification mentioned in the Act is customary in a profession, trade, vocation or calling`

§ `24. Evidence of pecuniary resources or property`

-- 24(2): the accused is "deemed to be in possession" of resources or property, or to have obtained an accretion, held or obtained by ANOTHER
-- person whom, "having regard to his relationship to the accused person or to any other circumstances, there is reason to believe" to be
-- holding them "in trust for or on behalf of the accused person or as a gift from the accused person".
@ref "Prevention of Corruption Act 1960, s 24(2)"
GIVEN e IS A `Corruption Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused is treated as holding the resources, property or accretion` IF
        NOT e's `the resources, property or accretion are held or obtained by another person`
    OR  e's `there is reason to believe, having regard to that person's relationship to the accused or to other circumstances, that the other person holds them in trust for or on behalf of the accused or as a gift from the accused`

-- 24(1): in a trial or inquiry into an offence under the Act (or under sections 161 to 165 or 213 to 215 of the Penal Code 1871, or a
-- conspiracy, attempt or abetment of one) the fact that the accused (a) is in possession, for which he cannot satisfactorily account, of
-- resources or property disproportionate to his known sources of income, or (b) had at or about the time of the alleged offence obtained an
-- accretion for which he cannot satisfactorily account, "may be proved and may be taken into consideration by the court as corroborating the
-- testimony of any witness" that the accused accepted or obtained or agreed to accept or attempted to obtain a gratification, "and as
-- showing that the gratification was accepted ... corruptly".  It is admissible and may be weighed; it is not a presumption and does not
-- shift a burden (contrast section 8).
@ref "Prevention of Corruption Act 1960, s 24(1) and (2)"
GIVEN e IS A `Corruption Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may take the disproportionate resources into consideration as corroboration` IF
        e's `the trial or inquiry is into an offence under this Act or under sections 161 to 165 or 213 to 215 of the Penal Code 1871, or into a conspiracy, attempt or abetment of one`
    AND (   e's `the accused is in possession of pecuniary resources or property disproportionate to his or her known sources of income for which he or she cannot satisfactorily account`
         OR e's `the accused obtained at or about the time of the alleged offence an accretion to pecuniary resources or property for which he or she cannot satisfactorily account`)
    AND `the accused is treated as holding the resources, property or accretion` e

§ `25. Evidence of accomplice`

-- 25: "Notwithstanding any rule of law or written law to the contrary, no witness shall, in any such trial or inquiry as is referred to in
-- section 24, be presumed to be unworthy of credit by reason only of any payment or delivery by him or on his behalf of any gratification
-- to an agent or member of a public body."  "By reason only": the witness may still be disbelieved on other grounds.
@ref "Prevention of Corruption Act 1960, s 25"
GIVEN e IS A `Corruption Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the witness may not be presumed unworthy of credit by reason only of the payment` IF
        e's `the trial or inquiry is into an offence under this Act or under sections 161 to 165 or 213 to 215 of the Penal Code 1871, or into a conspiracy, attempt or abetment of one`
    AND e's `the witness paid or delivered, or had paid or delivered on his or her behalf, a gratification to an agent or a member of a public body`

§ `36. Protection of informers`

-- 36(1): "no complaints as to an offence under this Act shall be admitted in evidence in any civil or criminal proceeding whatsoever, and no
-- witness shall be obliged or permitted to disclose the name or address of any informer, or state any matter which might lead to his
-- discovery", "except as hereinafter provided".  36(3) is the exception: (a) on a trial for an offence under the Act, the court, after full
-- inquiry, is of the opinion that the informer wilfully made in his complaint a material statement which he knew or believed to be false or
-- did not believe to be true; or (b) in any other proceeding, the court is of the opinion that justice cannot be fully done between the
-- parties without the discovery of the informer.  Then the court may require the original complaint, if in writing, and permit inquiry and
-- require full disclosure.  The court's concealment of entries in books and papers (36(2)) goes as far as necessary "but no further".
@ref "Prevention of Corruption Act 1960, s 36(3)(a) and (b)"
GIVEN e IS A `Corruption Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may require disclosure concerning the informer under section 36(3)` IF
        (   e's `the proceeding is a trial for an offence under this Act`
        AND e's `the informer wilfully made in his or her complaint a material statement which he or she knew or believed to be false or did not believe to be true, and the court, after full inquiry into the case, is of that opinion`)
    OR  (   NOT e's `the proceeding is a trial for an offence under this Act`
        AND e's `in a proceeding that is not a trial for an offence under this Act, the court is of the opinion that justice cannot be fully done between the parties without the discovery of the informer`)

@ref "Prevention of Corruption Act 1960, s 36(1)"
GIVEN e IS A `Corruption Evidence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a witness may be required to disclose the name or address of the informer` IF
    `the court may require disclosure concerning the informer under section 36(3)` e
